    {
        "content": "<p>Takashiidobe <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/4734#issuecomment-6221484710\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/issues/4734\">issue #4734</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was combing through the docs and found it interesting the docs don't mention the return type for snarrow at the very least:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Inputs</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">x</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SIMD</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">vector</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">containing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">integer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lanes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">32</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">or</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bits</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">wide</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">SIMD</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">vector</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">containing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">integer</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">lanes</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">16</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">32</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">or</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">bits</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">wide</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">Outputs</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">a</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I tried out this code on my <a href=\"https://clif-explorer.takashiidobe.com/?syntax=intel#code=ZnVuY3Rpb24gJW5hcnJvdyhpMTZ4MiwgaTE2eDIpIC0-IGk4eDQgewpibG9jazAodjA6IGkxNngyLCB2MTogaTE2eDIpOgogICAgdjIgPSBzbmFycm93LmkxNngyIHYwLCB2MQogICAgcmV0dXJuIHYyCn0K&amp;version=0.136.2&amp;arch=riscv64&amp;tab=clif&amp;flags=unwind_info=false&amp;fn=%25narrow&amp;args=%5B%22%5B300,+400%5D%22,%22%5B500,+600%5D%22%5D\">cranelift 0.136.2 version</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"clif\"><pre><span></span><code>function %narrow(i16x2, i16x2) -&gt; i8x4 {\nblock0(v0: i16x2, v1: i16x2):\n    v2 = snarrow.i16x2 v0, v1\n    return v2\n}\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>RV64 properly handles this, and I get the expected narrowing and widening of vectors. x86_64 and aarch64 indicate an isle lowering rule is missing, and s390x just hits an unreachable and crashes. </p>\n<p>For the i64x2 version, looks like only x86_64 is missing, like the issue originally reports. </p>\n</blockquote>",
